    "result_text": "All tests pass and the build succeeds. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented optional, region-aware postcode validation for owner creation, following the existing Spring conventions in the codebase.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added an optional `postcode` field to the shared `OwnerFields` schema (not in the `required` list), so the generated `OwnerFieldsDto` (request) and `OwnerDto` (response) both carry it. This keeps the request contract backward-compatible.\n\n2. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 Added the `postcode` field (`@Column(name = \"postcode\")`, nullable) with getter/setter.\n\n3. **DB schemas** (`db/{h2,hsqldb,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`) \u2014 Added a nullable `postcode` column to the `owners` table so JPA/Hibernate can persist it. (The test profile uses `hsqldb`.)\n\n4. **`validation/PostcodeValidator.java`** (new) \u2014 Validates only *when present* (a `null` postcode is skipped). When present it must be exactly 4 digits; if the city maps to a known region (Sydney\u2192NSW, Melbourne\u2192VIC, Brisbane\u2192QLD) the value must fall in that region's fixed inclusive range (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099). A city with no known region accepts any 4-digit postcode.\n\n5. **`validation/InvalidPostcodeException.java`** (new) + handler in **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 Translates an invalid/out-of-range postcode into `400 Bad Request`, mirroring the existing address/email/telephone handlers.\n\n6. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 Invokes `PostcodeValidator.validate(...)` alongside the other normalizers. MapStruct auto-maps `postcode` in both directions (matching names), so it is stored and returned when given.\n\n**Test results:** `Tests run: 3, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 valid postcode stored/returned, owner without postcode accepted, malformed postcode rejected with 400.",
