    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is structured exactly the way this codebase already organizes input handling, and it's cleanly additive:\n\n- **Right unit for the logic.** `PostcodeValidator` sits in `rest.validation` alongside the existing `AddressNormalizer`, `TelephoneNormalizer`, and `PetAgeValidator`. It mirrors their established shape precisely: `final` class, private constructor, static entry point, static `Map` lookup tables as constants, and a thorough class-level Javadoc. The `Validator` (rather than `Normalizer`) suffix is appropriate since it only checks \u2014 never mutates \u2014 and it's already precedented by `PetAgeValidator`.\n- **Exception + advice follow the existing seam.** `InvalidPostcodeException extends RuntimeException` matches `InvalidAddressException`/`InvalidTelephoneException`, and the new `@ExceptionHandler` in `ExceptionControllerAdvice` is a faithful copy of the sibling handlers (same logging, `detailBuild`, 400 status). No new error-translation mechanism was invented.\n- **Wiring is in the natural place.** `PostcodeValidator.validate(...)` is called in `addOwner` right next to the other normalize/validate calls, keeping the request-preparation sequence in one readable block. Nothing became a catch-all.\n- **No duplication.** The city\u2192region and region\u2192range tables live only in the validator; the controller stays thin and delegates.\n- **Model/schema/OpenAPI additions are conventional.** The `postcode` field, getter/setter, `@Column`, the four DB schema columns, and the OpenAPI property all follow the surrounding field patterns.\n\nA maintainer of this repo would find this a natural extension of the existing validation package.",
